The '''2022 Watford Borough Council election''' took place on 5 May 2022.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Patel |first=Dimple |title=Elections in Watford |url=https://www.watford.gov.uk/elections-voting/elections-watford-1 |access-date=2022-03-07 |website=www.watford.gov.uk |language=en}}</ref> One third (12) of the 36 members of [[Watford Borough Council]] were elected. The elections took place alongside [[2022 United Kingdom local elections|elections to local authorities across the United Kingdom]], and an election for the [[Mayor of Watford]].

The [[Liberal Democrats (UK)|Liberal Democrats]] went into the election controlling the council, holding 24 of the 36 seats. They also held the mayorship, after [[Peter Taylor (mayor)|Peter Taylor]] was elected in [[2018 Watford Borough Council election|2018]].<ref>{{Cite news |date=2018-05-04 |title=Vote 2018: Watford mayoral election result |language=en-GB |work=BBC News |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-politics-43827816 |access-date=2022-03-07}}</ref>

== Electoral process ==
{{see also|Watford Borough Council elections}}

Watford Borough Council is elected in thirds, with a third of all seats up for election every year for three years, with an election to [[Hertfordshire County Council]] instead in the fourth year.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/government/uploads/system/uploads/attachment_data/file/920186/Election_Timetable_in_England_2019.pdf|title=Election Timetable in England|access-date=31 March 2022|website=[[gov.uk]]}}</ref> The election took place by [[first-past-the-post voting]], with wards represented by three councillors, with one elected in each election year to serve a four-year term. The seats up for election in 2022 are those last contested in the [[2018 Watford Borough Council election|2018 election]].

